## 9. SECURITY

### 9.1 Authentication & Authorization

| Layer | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| **Studio Gate** | Password in `sessionStorage` (blocks random visitors) |
| **User Auth** | Firebase Google OAuth (2-factor via Google) |
| **Admin Routes** | `ProtectedRoute.tsx` — redirects to `/login` if not authenticated |
| **Database** | Firestore Security Rules enforce `studioId` isolation |

### 9.2 Firestore Security Rules

The security rules ensure:
- Only authenticated users can read studio data
- A user can ONLY access their own studio's data (multi-tenant isolation)
- Booking hard-delete is disabled (`allow delete: if false`) — must use soft delete
- Public enquiries can be created by anyone (for the public form)
- Gallery images are publicly readable but only admins can modify

```
// Key rule examples:
match /bookings/{bookingId} {
  // Can only create if the booking's studioId matches your studioId
  allow create: if isAuth() && request.resource.data.studioId == userStudio();
  // Can only read if the booking belongs to your studio
  allow read: if isAuth() && resource.data.studioId == userStudio();
  // Direct deletes are BLOCKED — must use soft delete
  allow delete: if false;
}
```

### 9.3 Data Protection

- [.env](file:///c:/Users/Play%20Edit%20Solution/Desktop/Cameraman%20Pro/.env) file stores all API keys (excluded from Git via `.gitignore`)
- No server storing client data — Firebase handles security at the infrastructure level
- Amounts stored as integers (paise) to prevent float precision bugs
- All Firestore writes go through [sanitizeFirestoreData()](file:///c:/Users/Play%20Edit%20Solution/Desktop/Cameraman%20Pro/src/hooks/useBookings.ts#20-58) to remove `undefined` values (which would cause Firestore errors)

### 9.4 Common Vulnerabilities & Prevention

| Vulnerability | Prevention |
|---|---|
| Unauthorized data access | Firestore rules check studioId per document |
| SQL Injection | Not applicable — NoSQL Firestore with typed SDK |
| XSS attacks | React auto-escapes JSX output |
| API key exposure | Keys in [.env](file:///c:/Users/Play%20Edit%20Solution/Desktop/Cameraman%20Pro/.env) with `VITE_` prefix (exposed to browser — note: Cloudinary and Groq keys ARE browser-visible, security via Cloudinary upload presets + server-side rate limits) |
| Session hijacking | Firebase JWT tokens with 1-hour expiry + refresh tokens |
| CSRF | Not applicable — direct Firebase SDK, no cookie sessions |

> [!WARNING]
> The Groq and Cloudinary API keys are visible in the browser bundle because there is no backend server. This is a known trade-off for serverless architectures. In production, a backend proxy should be used for sensitive operations.

